WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.731
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll

00:00:03.731 --> 00:00:06.920
define a class with fields, initialize objects

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:10.109
with a constructor, and use an instance method

00:00:10.109 --> 00:00:12.466
to change each object's own state.

00:00:13.116 --> 00:00:16.497
These skills let applications manage separate

00:00:16.497 --> 00:00:19.804
customer accounts, equipment loans, or order

00:00:19.804 --> 00:00:23.110
items with one shared set of rules, reducing

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:26.341
copied code as the number of records grows.

00:00:26.984 --> 00:00:30.266
A campus welcome desk needs a pass record for

00:00:30.266 --> 00:00:31.214
each visitor.

00:00:31.214 --> 00:00:34.714
Each pass stores a name and a whole-number visit

00:00:34.714 --> 00:00:36.610
count that starts at zero.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:39.819
Recording a visit adds one to that visitor's

00:00:39.819 --> 00:00:42.955
count; it must leave other visitors' counts

00:00:42.955 --> 00:00:43.684
unchanged.

00:00:44.334 --> 00:00:47.186
We'll define the pass, create two separate

00:00:47.186 --> 00:00:50.445
records, and report each visitor's name with the

00:00:50.445 --> 00:00:51.259
right count.

00:00:51.259 --> 00:00:54.382
After a worked example, you'll predict another

00:00:54.382 --> 00:00:57.234
pair of records before we run the program.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.718
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:01:00.718 --> 00:01:01.520
Main.java.

00:01:05.597 --> 00:01:08.672
The name and visit count belong together.

00:01:08.672 --> 00:01:12.197
A class defines the data and operations for one

00:01:12.197 --> 00:01:13.322
kind of object.

00:01:13.322 --> 00:01:16.772
Let's define DeskPass and give it two instance

00:01:16.772 --> 00:01:19.772
fields: an owner name and a visit count.

00:01:24.958 --> 00:01:28.369
These fields describe what each pass will store.

00:01:28.369 --> 00:01:31.780
A field is a variable that belongs to an object,

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:34.480
and the values together are its state.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:37.819
The class definition alone creates no visitor's

00:01:37.819 --> 00:01:38.175
pass.

00:01:38.175 --> 00:01:41.372
We also need a constructor to initialize each

00:01:41.372 --> 00:01:44.783
new pass with its owner's name and a zero count.

00:01:52.177 --> 00:01:55.510
The constructor's name matches DeskPass, and its

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:57.315
header has no return type.

00:01:57.315 --> 00:02:00.301
It receives an owner parameter for this one

00:02:00.301 --> 00:02:00.648
call.

00:02:00.648 --> 00:02:03.912
In the assignment, this dot owner names the new

00:02:03.912 --> 00:02:07.106
object's field, while owner on the right names

00:02:07.106 --> 00:02:08.564
that temporary input.

00:02:08.564 --> 00:02:11.549
The next assignment stores zero in the same

00:02:11.549 --> 00:02:13.077
object's visits field.

00:02:13.093 --> 00:02:16.327
Once a pass exists, we need to record visits

00:02:16.327 --> 00:02:17.944
without starting over.

00:02:17.944 --> 00:02:21.398
An instance method performs an operation on the

00:02:21.398 --> 00:02:23.602
object that receives the call.

00:02:23.602 --> 00:02:26.616
Let's add a visit method that updates the

00:02:26.616 --> 00:02:27.718
existing count.

00:02:33.956 --> 00:02:36.424
This refers to the current object.

00:02:36.424 --> 00:02:39.908
The assignment reads that object's visits field,

00:02:39.908 --> 00:02:43.320
adds one, and stores the new number back in the

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:43.755
field.

00:02:43.755 --> 00:02:47.094
The updated state remains after the call ends.

00:02:47.094 --> 00:02:50.215
Void means the method returns no value; its

00:02:50.215 --> 00:02:52.756
useful effect is the changed count.

00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:55.084
Our pass definition is complete.

00:02:55.084 --> 00:02:58.262
Below it, the Main class will hold main, the

00:02:58.262 --> 00:03:00.646
starting method for this program.

00:03:00.646 --> 00:03:03.969
That is where we'll create the passes and call

00:03:03.969 --> 00:03:05.197
their operations.

00:03:11.646 --> 00:03:14.938
Let's create one pass for Eden and another for

00:03:14.938 --> 00:03:15.296
Noah.

00:03:15.296 --> 00:03:18.732
Each new expression creates a separate DeskPass,

00:03:18.732 --> 00:03:22.096
invokes its constructor with a name, and yields

00:03:22.096 --> 00:03:25.245
a reference that lets us reach the resulting

00:03:25.245 --> 00:03:25.746
object.

00:03:34.619 --> 00:03:37.981
First and second now describe the references our

00:03:37.981 --> 00:03:39.172
program will use.

00:03:39.172 --> 00:03:41.904
When the program runs, each constructor

00:03:41.904 --> 00:03:44.846
initializes its own object's name and zero

00:03:44.846 --> 00:03:45.267
count.

00:03:45.267 --> 00:03:48.629
The constructor itself returns no value; the new

00:03:48.629 --> 00:03:51.011
expression provides the reference.

00:03:51.011 --> 00:03:53.953
Using the same class does not make the two

00:03:53.953 --> 00:03:55.844
objects share their fields.

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:59.009
We'll record one visit through first and two

00:03:59.009 --> 00:04:00.580
visits through second.

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:03.436
The reference before the dot selects the

00:04:03.436 --> 00:04:06.577
receiver: the particular object whose method

00:04:06.577 --> 00:04:06.934
runs.

00:04:06.934 --> 00:04:09.933
Each call uses the same visit rule on that

00:04:09.933 --> 00:04:11.218
receiver's fields.

00:04:18.651 --> 00:04:21.905
When these calls run, first's count goes from

00:04:21.905 --> 00:04:22.772
zero to one.

00:04:22.772 --> 00:04:26.171
The two calls on second move its separate count

00:04:26.171 --> 00:04:28.340
from zero to one, then to two.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:31.666
During each call, this refers to its receiver.

00:04:31.666 --> 00:04:35.064
Updating one receiver does not update the other

00:04:35.064 --> 00:04:35.426
pass.

00:04:35.448 --> 00:04:38.637
To check the records, let's print each owner's

00:04:38.637 --> 00:04:41.618
name beside the count read through the same

00:04:41.618 --> 00:04:42.312
reference.

00:04:42.312 --> 00:04:45.224
The dot selects a field, so each label and

00:04:45.224 --> 00:04:47.373
number come from the same pass.

00:05:00.901 --> 00:05:03.829
The complete program creates both passes,

00:05:03.829 --> 00:05:06.970
records the visits, and prints their current

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:07.399
state.

00:05:07.399 --> 00:05:10.684
Let's click Run and check that the two records

00:05:10.684 --> 00:05:11.826
stayed separate.

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:21.446
Eden is one because first received one visit

00:05:21.446 --> 00:05:21.814
call.

00:05:21.814 --> 00:05:24.756
Noah is two because second received two.

00:05:24.756 --> 00:05:28.140
Both used the same method definition, but each

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:31.008
call changed only its receiver's field.

00:05:31.008 --> 00:05:34.539
The constructor supplied the starting state once

00:05:34.539 --> 00:05:36.010
for each new object.

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:40.314
Now let's use the same pass definition for Maya

00:05:40.314 --> 00:05:43.058
and Luis with a different call sequence.

00:05:43.058 --> 00:05:46.145
We'll replace the worked program with a fresh

00:05:46.145 --> 00:05:46.488
copy.

00:05:46.488 --> 00:05:49.780
The fields, constructor, and visit rule stay the

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:53.004
same, so you can focus on which object receives

00:05:53.004 --> 00:05:53.690
each call.

00:06:11.936 --> 00:06:13.685
The class is ready again.

00:06:13.685 --> 00:06:16.974
We'll add Main and create two fresh objects for

00:06:16.974 --> 00:06:17.954
Maya and Luis.

00:06:17.954 --> 00:06:20.823
These new expressions start a new pair of

00:06:20.823 --> 00:06:24.181
records; they do not reuse the earlier visitors'

00:06:24.181 --> 00:06:25.161
stored counts.

00:06:41.289 --> 00:06:44.561
The two object-creation statements are entered.

00:06:44.561 --> 00:06:47.415
Let's add the visit calls and the report.

00:06:47.415 --> 00:06:50.617
Follow the reference before each dot to decide

00:06:50.617 --> 00:06:53.819
which count changes, then keep your prediction

00:06:53.819 --> 00:06:56.464
until the complete program is visible.

00:07:17.419 --> 00:07:20.566
Before we run it, predict both output lines.

00:07:20.566 --> 00:07:23.428
Begin with the counts established by the

00:07:23.428 --> 00:07:26.504
constructors and trace the receiver of each

00:07:26.504 --> 00:07:27.290
visit call.

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:29.794
Pause here if you'd like more time.

00:07:37.885 --> 00:07:41.546
Let's click Run and compare the report with your

00:07:41.546 --> 00:07:42.385
prediction.

00:07:47.936 --> 00:07:51.155
Maya is two because first receives two visit

00:07:51.155 --> 00:07:51.594
calls.

00:07:51.594 --> 00:07:54.520
Luis is one because second receives one.

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:57.885
Both counts began at zero in separate objects.

00:07:57.885 --> 00:08:01.177
The final print statements read each object's

00:08:01.177 --> 00:08:04.469
own name and count, so the result agrees with

00:08:04.469 --> 00:08:05.786
the call sequence.

00:08:06.525 --> 00:08:09.422
The desk now has separate visitor records

00:08:09.422 --> 00:08:11.329
governed by one definition.

00:08:11.329 --> 00:08:14.721
Fields hold each object's state, the constructor

00:08:14.721 --> 00:08:17.547
establishes its starting values, and the

00:08:17.547 --> 00:08:20.797
instance method applies the shared update rule

00:08:20.797 --> 00:08:21.927
to its receiver.

00:08:21.927 --> 00:08:24.895
We can change that rule in one place while

00:08:24.895 --> 00:08:27.650
keeping every visitor's count separate.

00:08:28.959 --> 00:08:31.790
Suppose the desk creates a third pass but

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:33.447
records no visits on it.

00:08:33.447 --> 00:08:36.692
What would its count be, and would another call

00:08:36.692 --> 00:08:38.349
through first change it?

00:08:38.349 --> 00:08:41.594
Explain which object this would refer to during

00:08:41.594 --> 00:08:42.284
that call.
